# Event record schema

Every row in the `events` table must include:

- `city` — affected city (Ottawa, Toronto, Vancouver).
- `event_type` — snake_case identifier for the detector, e.g.
  `rapid_temperature_change`. Globally unique per detector.
- `description` — one human-readable sentence explaining what happened,
  in which city, and the numeric reason it fired.
- `severity` — exactly one of `"low"`, `"medium"`, `"high"`.
- `reading_id` — id of the row in `readings` that triggered this event.
  May be `None` if the event has no single triggering reading.
- `reading_data` — JSON string of the raw values that explain why the
  event fired. Pass it via the `metadata={...}` parameter of
  `store_event()`; it is JSON-encoded for you.
- `detected_at` — UTC ISO 8601 timestamp; use
  `datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()`.

# Detector conventions

- Never write to the `events` table directly — always go through
  `store_event()` in `events.py`.
- Return early with a guard clause when insufficient history exists
  (e.g. `if len(readings) < 10: return` in `check_temperature_anomaly`).
- Detectors SHOULD fire on state transitions, not repeatedly while a
  condition persists. When proposing a new detector, justify the dedup
  strategy: transition check (current vs previous), time-windowed
  cooldown (no refire within N hours for the same event_type and city),
  or one-shot absolute (fires once, never again — discouraged for
  recurring conditions).
- When adding a new detector, register it inside `check_for_events()`
  so it runs on each new reading.